With YOYO_ASK_OUTSIDE_READS=1 set, a search or list_files whose root resolves outside the working directory now asks y/n on a terminal, and is refused without one, just like read_file. Commit 796e92cf, part of #879. cargo fmt --check, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings and the full cargo test all pass.

How it was done
• Only `src/outside_read.rs` needed code changes. GuardedTool and ArcGuardedTool already pass every guarded tool's path to outside_read::check, and search and list_files already go through that guard. The fence just ignored any tool not named read_file. tool_wrappers.rs stays at 5684 lines, so the size gate is untouched.
• Same rules as `read_file`:
  • paths are resolved with symlinks followed;
  • a root that doesn't exist isn't asked about;
  • nothing is asked when --allow-dir is set;
  • no root means the current directory, which is inside.
• The default is unchanged. With the env var unset, all three tools behave exactly as before, and a test pins that.

One decision the plan didn't specify: a yes is now remembered as one of two kinds. Both tools search subdirectories too, so a "yes" for reading ~/.bashrc must not also allow a search rooted at ~, which would reach ~/.ssh.
• A file target (any read_file, or a root that is a file) is remembered as its parent directory, and that approval is shared with read_file.
• A directory root is remembered as exactly that directory and everything under it. Approving /a/b does not cover a later root at /a/b/c.
• The two kinds never cover each other, and the prompt says "(recursive)" for the directory kind.
• read_file's prompt and refusal text are unchanged.

Tests and check: four new tests cover an outside root being asked about or refused, inside roots passing (., a subdirectory, a file, a symlink pointing inside), a symlink inside the directory that points outside counting as outside, and the remembered keys staying exact and separate. To check the tests can fail, I put the fence back to read_file only, ran them, and restored it in one command. Exactly the three outside-root tests failed by name, and everything passed again after the restore.

Docs and issue: docs/src/configuration/permissions.md now names the three covered tools and says plainly that bash and file writes are not covered. The commit body lists every built-in tool that takes a path and whether it's covered. I commented on #879 with the same list. Still open there: there's no CLI flag or .yoyo.toml key (the env var is the only switch), the default-on decision (item 3) hasn't been made, and bash is still uncovered.
